    "text": "To determine Melony's total profit from selling 7 shirts and 3 pairs of sandals, we need to break down the problem into smaller steps.\n\nFirst, let's find out how much profit Melony makes from selling one shirt. We know that she makes $21 of profit every time she sells 3 shirts. Therefore, the profit per shirt is:\n\\[\n\\frac{21}{3} = 7 \\text{ dollars}\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find out how much profit she makes from selling one pair of sandals. We know that she makes four times as much profit from selling two pairs of sandals as she does from selling 3 shirts. Since she makes $21 from 3 shirts, she makes:\n\\[\n4 \\times 21 = 84 \\text{ dollars} \\text{ from two pairs of sandals}\n\\]\nSo, the profit per pair of sandals is:\n\\[\n\\frac{84}{2} = 42 \\text{ dollars}\n\\]\n\nNow, we can calculate the total profit from selling 7 shirts. Since each shirt sells for $7, the profit from 7 shirts is:\n\\[\n7 \\times 7 = 49 \\text{ dollars}\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate the total profit from selling 3 pairs of sandals. Since each pair of sandals sells for $42, the profit from 3 pairs of sandals is:\n\\[\n3 \\times 42 = 126 \\text{ dollars}\n\\]\n\nFinally, we add the profit from the shirts and the profit from the sandals to get the total profit:\n\\[\n49 + 126 = 175 \\text{ dollars}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the total profit Melony will make if she sells 7 shirts and 3 pairs of sandals is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{175}\n\\]",
